@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Azure Quickstart Center provides popular services";
    Layout = "~/Areas/Azure/Views/Shared/AzureLayout.cshtml";
}

@await Html.PartialAsync("~/Areas/Azure/Views/Shared/AzureContentStyles.cshtml")

<div class="row g-4">
    <aside class="col-lg-3">
        @Html.Partial("~/Areas/Azure/Views/Shared/AzureTopics.cshtml")
    </aside>
    <section class="col-lg-9">
        <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm">
            <div class="card-body p-4 p-lg-5">
                <div class="azure-notes-content">
                    <pre class="azure-notes-text">Azure Quickstart Center provides popular services, tutorials, and guides to get started in your Azure
journey. To find Azure Quickstart Center, you can find it in the default list of services on the Azure
Portal homepage. If you don&#x27;t see Azure Quickstart Center in the Azure services section of the portal
home page, you can also navigate to the search tab and find the Azure Quickstart Center by typing in
Quickstart Center.




The Azure Quickstart Center aims to make it easy to start a project. It provides services and goals,
setup guides, as well as online courses to help us get started on our Azure journey




Let&#x27;s start with creating a project. Click on deploy a virtual machine. You can see two types of virtual
machines shown—a Windows virtual machine and a Linux virtual machine.
If you know which virtual machine you want, you can click the create button.

However, if you want more information about the service, click the learn more tab on each service
found at the bottom of the card. The card will expand to reveal more information. Here you&#x27;ll find
some relevant information, such as an expanded explanation and reference architecture which will
help understand a typical scenario of how a service will work with other services in Azure. It’ll also
provide you with a five-minute Quickstart tutorial, as well as recommended SKUs and pricing options
to get you started and use your free trial credit.
After learning the given information on the page to create your service, you can click the create
button. The button will take you to a prefilled template for the service. You may also need to fill in
the required information to create the service. Here, you can see a bunch of different options to help
you customize and create the virtual machine that&#x27;s perfectly fit for you and your organization.
You do have recommended items, such as the size. So, click on see all sizes. If you&#x27;ll recall, the Azure
Quickstart Center provides B1S as a recommended size to select for your free trial, select that.

Next, fill out all the required information from the resource group, the virtual machine, region, so on
and so forth. If you want, you can continue to customize by clicking on the different tabs or you can
also, go through a walkthrough menu by clicking next.

Once you are ready with all of your customization and options that are right for you, you can hit
review and create. You&#x27;ve created your first service using Azure Quickstart Center.</pre>
                    <div class="text-center my-4">
